Andy Cohen reflects on the Orlando  shooting it just kills me and so  a late night host sat down with ETS  executive producer Brad Bessie on Monday  shortly after he attended a vigil at the  Stonewall Inn in New York City where he  honored those who lost their lives and  read several of their names out loud I'm  a gay American and I'm a gay New Yorker  and there was a vigil in my neighborhood  that I went to as a citizen I didn't  expect to take part in it I just wanted  to pay tribute to to those people the 48  year old got emotional talking about the  horrific massacre and the effect its had  on him this has touched me probably more  than anything just because I think I  could have been there and when I look at  the pictures of the victims I see their  faces and I see people that I know and  that I relate to and you go to a gay bar  and for some people it's the only safe  place that they have so the idea that  these people who were there to be just  with people that they loved and be safe  and have fun were you know killed being  who they are is just it just kills me I  think you know this was an attack on gay  people by a raging lunatic who hated  people like me it's a scary scary  reminder that there are really off of  people in the world Andy said the  tragedy has compelled him to take action  against gun violence nobody needs an  assault rifle except the people who are  actually protecting us it made me feel  like I'm gonna do what I can as a  citizen to get rid of assault weapons I  think we all have a responsibility to  raise our voices I am very proud to be  the only gay host on late-night  television and I don't classify my show  as a gay talk show I happen to begin  it's one of the things I happen to be  just as all those people  in Orlando that was one of the things  they happen to be they happen to be  killed for for being gay and that kills  me
 

